# The Hosted Child's Quiescence — measure locally, nudge over the bus, and put idle on the glass [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

> The console can host a live `claude` child inside a widget. The predecessor host, `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py`,
> has run a two-tier quiescence heartbeat over its child since BUG 3, and the two-tier shape exists only because a
> single fixed tier failed in production. The starting question was whether the widget-hosted child had that
> heartbeat at all. It does — [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY] shipped the wiring on 2026-08-11 and the code confirms it.
> What it does not have is any way to tell whether the heartbeat runs at the cadence anyone intended, whether its
> nudges arrive, or how long the child has actually been quiet. A heartbeat nobody can observe is precisely the
> condition that let BUG 3 run for about eight hours and forty-eight pings before a human caught it by hand. The
> load-bearing content below is a DECOMPOSITION: measurement and nudging were fused only because one process did
> both, and separating them is what makes the mechanism reusable at the next host.

---

## 1. What was measured, and what the starting hypothesis got wrong

### [CXC-CQ-AS-BUILT] The hosted child's quiescence heartbeat EXISTS — the gap is observability, not absence

- claim: the widget-hosted child DOES run the predecessor's two-tier quiescence heartbeat, through the same class
  and the same factory. The hypothesis that it has no idle tracking is refuted by the code as of 2026-08-13, so
  every requirement in this spec is an OBSERVABILITY requirement over a live mechanism, not a request to build one.
- evidence: `python/composeio/render/widgets/terminal.py` — `_start_monitor` (line 967) calls the shared
  `ptycore.build_monitor` and starts `HarnessMonitor.start_thread()` (line 990); `start_session` calls it at line
  872 and `stop_session` tears it down at line 1067; `_on_child_bytes` (line 476) calls `mon.note_activity()` on the
  reader thread at line 487; a `{"cmd":"context"}` query reaches `self._monitor.handle_query()` at line 1123.
- evidence: `python/composeio/ptycore.py::build_monitor` (line 1628) passes the FULL two-tier cadence —
  `heartbeat_base_s`, `heartbeat_backoff_after_s` and `heartbeat_then_s` from `load_heartbeat_cadence`, plus a live
  `cadence_provider` and `echo_grace_s`. The hosted child therefore inherits both scars intact: the two-tier
  escalation, and the self-echo guard that stops a nudge resetting the idle it measures.
- correction: the effective hosted default is NOT a single `600`. `heartbeat_idle_s = 600.0` is the LEGACY
  single-tier alias on `HarnessMonitor.__init__` (`monitor.py` line 105), superseded whenever the three-value
  cadence is supplied — which `build_monitor` always does. The real hosted default is
  `base 600 s / backoff_after 3600 s / then 3600 s`, i.e. 10 / 60 / 60 minutes (`ptycore.py` lines 1600-1602).
- correction: the seven `idle` and `timeout` occurrences in `terminal.py` ARE display state and I/O timeouts as
  measured, and `self._state = "idle"` (line 365) does mean "nothing is running here". That reading was right. The
  inference that therefore nothing measures quiescence was wrong, because the quiescence clock lives inside the
  monitor thread and is never spelled `idle` in the widget at all. That invisibility is [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE].
- depends-on: [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]
- see-also: [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-NOT-AWAY-WATCH] Two idle clocks exist and they measure different subjects — relate, never conflate

- constraint: [SPEC:CXC-IDLE-TRACKING] and its `CXC-IT-*` family measure the HUMAN's dead clock —
  `build_complete_ts` to `resumed_ts` — so the next away-watch queue can be sized empirically. This spec measures a
  CHILD INSTANCE's dead clock, time since its last genuine PTY output, so the harness can decide whether to nudge
  it. Different subject, different actor, different consumer, different storage.
- requirement: a build MUST NOT reuse the away-watch columns, the `away_watch` row, or the `self/away_watches`
  projection for hosted-child quiescence. Sharing the English word `idle` across the two is tolerable; sharing a
  field is not, because a query returning both would be answering two questions with one number.
- verify-machine: a grep guard asserting no hosted-child quiescence code path reads or writes `build_complete_ts`,
  `resumed_ts` or `idle_seconds` on the `away_watch` table.
- verify-human: David reads the away-watch sizing readout and a hosted child's quiescence readout side by side and
  can say in one sentence which is which.
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-IDLE-TRACKING]
- see-also: [CXC-IT-MEASURE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

---

## 2. The decomposition — measurement and nudging are separable

### [CXC-CQ-SPLIT] Measurement needs the byte stream; nudging needs only the bus

- claim: quiescence measurement and quiescence nudging are two mechanisms, not one. They were fused because the
  predecessor process happened to own both the PTY and the publish path, and that fusion is an artefact of one
  implementation rather than a property of the problem.
- constraint: MEASUREMENT is mechanical and local. Idle is defined as continuous idle since the last GENUINE child
  output ([CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]), so something must observe the child's actual bytes. Only the process holding
  the PTY master can do that, whichever host it is.
- constraint: NUDGING is semantic and remote. It is an addressed message to a peer asking whether it is still
  working. It requires no fd, no terminal, no emulator, and no co-location with the child — only the bus.
- consequence: the two halves have different placement constraints, different failure modes and different tests. A
  design that keeps them fused inherits the union of both constraint sets for no benefit, which is what makes the
  split the load-bearing content of this spec rather than a presentational choice.
- consequence: this WEAKENS [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST]. A monitor that nudges over the channel needs only the
  READ side of the PTY, which the widget's reader thread already provides; it never needs the write side. The
  where-does-the-monitor-live question shrinks to "where does the byte observer live", and the byte observer has
  exactly one possible home.
- consequence: separable is not the same as independently USEFUL. [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD] rules that on THIS
  surface — a child running `--ax-screen-reader` ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]) — measurement with no reachable peer to
  nudge has no consumer, so the monitor refuses to arm rather than measuring into the void. The split stays true of
  the ARCHITECTURE and pays off at the next host; it does not license instrumenting an unreachable one.
- requirement: the two halves MUST be independently testable. A test of the measurement half MUST be able to drive
  idle across the tiers with no bus at all, and a test of the nudge half MUST be able to assert the published
  message with no PTY at all. If either test needs both, they are still fused.
- verify-machine: two test modules exist, one importing no bus fake and one importing no PTY fake, and both pass.
- verify-human: David can state, without reading code, which half breaks when the bus is down and which half breaks
  when the child dies — the split is only real if it is legible.
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-AS-BUILT]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-MONITOR-LOCATION]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD]
- see-also: [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT] A nudge is a CHANNEL message, never a keystroke into the PTY

- claim: the nudge is delivered as an addressed bus message on the child's channel. Typing a nudge into the child's
  terminal is rejected as the mechanism — not deferred, not a fallback by default, rejected — and the reasons are
  four independent ones plus a platform-spine argument.
- rationale: (1) it never touches the PTY, so there is no injection into a terminal a human may also be typing
  into, no race with their keystrokes, and no escape-sequence hazard against a live TUI.
- rationale: (2) it is addressed and auditable. It carries who sent it and why, it lands on the bus where it can be
  observed, and it appears in the child's transcript AS A MESSAGE. A keystroke is anonymous input that nobody can
  later attribute to a sender.
- rationale: (3) the child can REASON about it — answer, defer, or explain why it is quiet. A keystroke can only be
  absorbed, which discards the one thing a language model is uniquely able to contribute to the question.
- rationale: (4) it is host-independent. A launcher-hosted instance, a widget-hosted instance and a break-glass
  terminal session are all bus peers, so one nudge mechanism serves all three.
- design-check: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s design-check — "a mechanism built for level N MUST be reusable at N+1; if
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]
  it is not, it was designed too specifically." The keystroke nudge FAILS that check: it is reusable only by a host
  that owns a PTY. The channel nudge PASSES it: it is reusable by any peer that can address the child. This is the
  strongest available argument for the design and it comes from the platform's own spine rather than from taste.
- consequence: [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]'s single-write-site and injection-log contracts are UNTOUCHED by this spec,
  because nothing specified here writes to a PTY. That is a feature: the audit property those contracts establish
  cannot be weakened by a change that never uses them.
- consequence: the nudge inherits the bus's delivery semantics, which means it inherits the bus's failure modes.
  What a nudge does when the child has no bridge behind its namespace is [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD], and the
  message's own shape is [DD-CQ-NUDGE-MESSAGE-SHAPE].
- verify-machine: a grep guard asserting no quiescence code path calls `inject`, `inject_line`, `write_keys` or
  `PtyChild._write`; a test asserting a fired heartbeat produces exactly one publish and zero PTY writes.
- verify-human: David watches a nudge arrive in a hosted child's transcript as a visible, attributable message and
  confirms it did not disturb what he was typing at the glass.
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-NUDGE-MESSAGE-SHAPE]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD] INVARIANT — a nudge and its echo MUST NOT reset the idle clock

- claim: this is settled by measurement and is NOT a fork. A nudge is ECHO, not genuine activity. Letting it reset
  the idle clock means the cadence can never escalate, because the nudge resets the very quantity it measures.
- evidence: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` lines 153-158, in its own words — child output within
  `echo_grace_s` of a harness self-emit "is treated as an echo, NOT genuine activity, so it does not reset the idle
  clock. Without this, the cadence can never escalate (the nudge resets the idle it measures): the recurrence of
  BUG 3." The mechanism is the single self-emit chokepoint `_emit` (line 184), which stamps `_last_self_emit`
  before every publish.
- requirement: the invariant holds for the channel nudge exactly as it held for the launcher's. Every path that
  emits a harness message to a hosted child MUST pass through the stamping chokepoint before publishing. A second
  emit site that publishes without stamping silently reintroduces BUG 3 and no test that drives `note_activity()`
  by hand will catch it — the prior fix shipped a green suite over exactly that hole.
- constraint: the invariant now spans a TRANSPORT BOUNDARY, which is a new way for it to fail. The launcher's
  self-emit and the child's echo were separated by one process and a PTY read. A channel nudge leaves this process,
  crosses the bus, is rendered by the child, and returns as PTY output — so the echo window must now absorb bus
  latency, bridge queuing and the child's render time, none of which the 90-second default was chosen against.
- requirement: an echo arriving back over the bus MUST be recognisable as the system's own nudge. The nudge message
  MUST carry an identifier the emitter can recognise on the return path, so that echo detection does not rest on a
  timing window alone. A time-only guard degrades silently under load: it does not fail, it just stops
  discriminating, which is the same shape as the defect it was written to prevent.
- default: `echo_grace_s` stays at 90 s until measured otherwise. The default is inherited, not endorsed — see
  [DD-CQ-CONSTANTS], which covers the cadence constants and this one together.
- scenario:
  - when: a nudge is published and the child renders a short reply to it
  - then: the reply does NOT reset the idle clock, and continuous idle keeps accumulating toward `backoff_after`
  - when: the child genuinely resumes work and produces sustained output past the echo window
  - then: the clock resets to zero and the cadence returns to the `base` tier
  - when: bus latency delays the echo past `echo_grace_s`
  - then: the carried identifier still marks it as an echo, and the clock still does not reset
- verify-machine: a test drives an emit-then-echo cycle under an injected clock and asserts continuous idle crosses
  `backoff_after` despite an echo on every nudge — the escalation that BUG 3's green suite failed to prove; a test
  delays the echo past `echo_grace_s` and asserts the identifier still suppresses the reset; a grep guard asserting
  exactly one self-emit chokepoint in the tree.
- verify-human: David leaves a hosted child quiet for longer than `backoff_after` and confirms on the glass that
  the cadence actually backed off rather than pinning at the fast tier — the exact observation that caught BUG 3.
- depends-on: [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-PEER-INJECT-IS-ACTIVITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

---

## 3. The four residual gaps

### [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-READBACK] The hosted child publishes no effective cadence, so SET-versus-CURRENT is dark for it

- premise: (measured) `ptycore.build_monitor` accepts a `publish_cadence` hook (line 1634). The launcher's wrapper
  passes one, publishing to `intelligence/<id>/config/heartbeat/current` (`launcher.py` lines 301-302).
  `TerminalWidget._start_monitor` (terminal.py lines 978-984) passes `intelligence_id`, `publish_in`, `jsonl_path`,
  `thresholds` and `log`, and does NOT pass `publish_cadence`. The hosted child therefore publishes nothing on its
  `config/heartbeat/current` topic.
- constraint: that readback is not decoration. [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE] records it as the fix for the
  observability gap that drove BUG 3's recurrence: with no readback of the cadence actually running, a cadence edit
  gives no feedback that it took, so a working edit and a dead one look identical. For a hosted child the situation
  is strictly worse than the launcher's pre-fix state, because there is not even a terminal log line to read.
- requirement: `TerminalWidget._start_monitor` MUST pass a `publish_cadence` hook that publishes the effective
  `base`, `backoff_after` and `then` — in MINUTES, matching the Config tab's set values — under the CHILD's id per
  [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]. On change only, never as a stream.
- requirement: a publish failure MUST be logged through `self._log` and never swallowed. A readback that can fail
  silently reintroduces the gap it exists to close.
- default: publish on change only, with the first publish emitted at monitor construction so a freshly mounted
  widget is never a blank readout.
- scenario:
  - when: a hosted child's monitor is constructed
  - then: exactly one cadence readback carries the three effective values in minutes, under the CHILD's id
  - when: David edits the cadence and `config_persister` writes it while the child runs
  - then: within one `poll_s` (default 20 s) the running monitor re-reads it via `cadence_provider` AND a new
    readback reflects the new value — set and current agree with no relaunch
- verify-machine: a test asserts `build_monitor` is called from the widget with a non-None `publish_cadence`; a test
  drives a cadence change through a fake `cadence_provider` and asserts exactly one new publish carrying the new
  values in minutes; a test asserts the topic carries the CHILD's id and not the host's.
- verify-human: David changes the cadence on the Config tab while a hosted child runs and watches the console's
  set-versus-current indicator reach agreement without relaunching anything.
- depends-on: [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-NUDGE-MESSAGE-SHAPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-IDENTITY] A hosted child's cadence resolves from a manifest that may not exist, and from the HOST's environment

- premise: (measured) `load_heartbeat_cadence(intelligence_id)` (`ptycore.py` line 1577) reads
  `default_meta_manifest(intelligence_id)` and, on any `OSError` or `ValueError`, falls through to the coded
  defaults with the exception swallowed and NOTHING logged (lines 1585-1599). A hosted child's instance id is minted
  per [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]; nothing guarantees an instance-scoped meta-object exists at that path.
- premise: (the identity leak) the same function then applies `HEARTBEAT_BASE_S`, the legacy `HEARTBEAT_IDLE_S`,
  `HEARTBEAT_BACKOFF_AFTER_S` and `HEARTBEAT_THEN_S` from `os.environ` (lines 1603-1613). For a hosted child that
  environment is the CONSOLE HOST's. One operator pin in the host's env therefore overrides the cadence of EVERY
  child the console hosts — a HOST-axis value silently beating an INSTANCE-axis one, which is symptom 2 of
  [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] wearing a new costume.
- requirement: the cadence source MUST be self-declaring. Whatever [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS] settles, the readback
  required by [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-READBACK] MUST carry a `source` field naming where each value came from — one of
  `instance-meta`, `role-meta`, `host-env` or `coded-default` — so a reader can distinguish a CONFIGURED 10/60/60
  from a DEFAULTED 10/60/60. Those two are indistinguishable today and they are not the same fact.
- requirement: a manifest that cannot be read MUST be logged through the monitor's `log` at construction, naming the
  path tried. A silent fall-through to defaults is the failure class this corpus has now paid for three times on
  [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE].
- constraint: this node does NOT settle which axis wins — that is [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS]. It settles only that
  whichever answer wins must be VISIBLE in the readback rather than inferred from surprise.
- scenario:
  - when: no meta-object exists for the child's instance id
  - then: the monitor logs the path it tried and the readback's `source` reads `coded-default`
  - when: `HEARTBEAT_BASE_S` is set in the console host's environment and two children are hosted
  - then: each child's readback names `host-env` as its source, so the shared pin is visible
- verify-machine: a test with a missing manifest asserts a log line naming the attempted path and a `coded-default`
  source; a test with `HEARTBEAT_BASE_S` in env asserts `host-env` for both of two hosted children; a test asserts
  a configured 10/60/60 and a defaulted 10/60/60 yield DIFFERENT `source` values.
- verify-human: David sets a cadence for one hosted child and confirms on the glass that a second hosted child's
  readout does not move with it.
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY] An undelivered nudge is silent, and the system then believes it acted

- premise: (measured) the widget's `publish_in` is `_publish_to_child` (terminal.py line 942). It publishes a
  harness envelope to the raw topic `intelligence/<child-id>/in` through the bus's foreign-channel seam, wrapped in
  a bare `except Exception` that logs `harness message DROPPED (bus down)` into a bounded in-memory list (lines
  959-965). Nothing on the glass, nothing on the bus, no counter — and the monitor's state advances as though the
  nudge landed.
- premise: (the sharper case, measured by the architect on 2026-08-13) publishing into a namespace behind which no
  channel bridge is bound fails WITHOUT raising. The console runs one `channel_bridge`, bound to a single instance
  id — `architect-hosted` is the id the shipped scene and `ptycore` both name. A peer whose id has no bridge behind
  it therefore publishes into a namespace with nothing listening, and the publish SUCCEEDS. A nudge that vanishes
  is worse than no nudge, because the idle clock keeps running and the system believes it acted.
- requirement: an undelivered nudge MUST NOT advance the heartbeat clock. Advancing `_last_heartbeat` on a nudge
  that did not land converts a broken delivery path into an apparently healthy quiet child, and the escalation to
  the `then` tier then fires against a child that never heard the `base` tier.
- requirement: the undelivered condition MUST be machine-readable, not merely logged. A bounded in-memory list that
  dies with the process cannot be asserted on, cannot be alerted on, and cannot be read from the glass; a condition
  visible only in a log is the shape that made an unoriented instance indistinguishable from an oriented one.
- requirement: what a bridgeless child DOES about it is SETTLED — [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD], ratified by David on
  2026-08-13: REFUSE TO ARM. The heartbeat half is not armed while no bridge is bound, so this node's rules govern
  the narrower surviving case: a bridge that was bound when the monitor armed and vanished before the next poll.
- constraint: delivery proof stronger than "the publish did not fail and a bridge is bound" is out of scope. The
  bus offers no per-message ack on this seam and inventing one is a larger change than this gap warrants. The bar
  is that a nudge into a namespace with nothing behind it is never counted as a nudge that landed.
- scenario:
  - when: a bridge was bound at arm time, vanishes, and a heartbeat fires before the next poll disarms
  - then: the condition is recorded machine-readably and the monitor's heartbeat clock does NOT advance
  - when: a bridge is bound behind the child's namespace and a heartbeat fires
  - then: the clock advances exactly once
- verify-machine: a test with a raising publish asserts the recorded condition and an unchanged `_last_heartbeat`; a
  test with a succeeding-but-unbound namespace asserts the SAME outcome, which is the case a naive test misses; a
  test asserts a bound, successful publish advances the clock exactly once.
- verify-human: David removes the bridge behind a hosted child, watches the console say so within one heartbeat
  interval, restores it, and watches the condition clear.
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE] The child's idle clock is private to a thread — nothing publishes it and nothing renders it

- premise: (measured) `HarnessMonitor` holds `_last_activity`, `_last_heartbeat` and `_last_self_emit` as private
  attributes on the monitor thread (`monitor.py` lines 171-179). The widget exposes only `monitor` as a property
  (terminal.py line 1002). No topic carries the child's idle seconds, no topic carries which tier it is in, and no
  widget draws either. A human looking at a hosted child cannot tell "working" from "quiet for two hours".
- requirement: the hosted child's quiescence MUST be published as retained state under `self/<child-id>/quiescence`,
  keyed on the CHILD's id, carrying at minimum `idle_seconds`, `tier` (`base` or `backed-off`), `cadence_s` and
  `last_heartbeat_ts`. This is the shape the launcher already writes to its log per check; the requirement is that
  it becomes a bus fact instead of a log line.
- constraint: publication is state-on-change, not a stream. An `idle_seconds` republished every tick is a
  per-second write per child, and [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE] is explicit that per-child work inside the render process is the
  cost class the entire widget budget exists to avoid. Publish on tier change, on heartbeat fire, and on the
  activity edge that resets the clock — three events, not a clock.
- default: the publish floor is `poll_s` (default 20 s); no quiescence publish is emitted more often than that
  regardless of how many edges occur.
- requirement: the readout MUST be renderable without the render thread computing it. The monitor publishes and a
  binding renders. No idle arithmetic on the draw path.
- scenario:
  - when: a hosted child produces no genuine output for longer than `backoff_after`
  - then: exactly one quiescence publish records the transition to `backed-off`, carrying the new `cadence_s`
  - when: the child resumes producing genuine output
  - then: one publish records `tier: base` with an `idle_seconds` near zero, and nothing further until the next edge
- verify-machine: a test drives the monitor under an injected clock across the `backoff_after` boundary and asserts
  exactly one transition publish rather than one per tick; a test asserts no publish occurs more often than
  `poll_s`; a test asserts the topic carries the CHILD's id.
- verify-human: David looks at a hosted child that has been quiet all afternoon and can read how long it has been
  quiet and whether the harness has backed off, without opening a log.
- depends-on: [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-DISPLAY-STATE-NAMING]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NOT-AWAY-WATCH]
- see-also: [DD-CQ-CONSTANTS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-DISPLAY-STATE-NAMING] The word `idle` already means the opposite on this widget's glass

- premise: (measured) `terminal.py` line 365 sets `self._state = "idle"` for a widget with NO child, and line 1425's
  `_idle_plate()` is the no-session plate. In the widget's vocabulary `idle` means "nothing is running here". In the
  quiescence vocabulary `idle` means "something is running here and has produced nothing for N seconds". Opposite
  conditions, one word, one surface.
- requirement: the quiescence readout MUST NOT be labelled `idle` on the glass or on the bus. The bus key is
  `quiescence` ([CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE]) and the glass label is `quiet for H:MM` or equivalent. The widget's existing
  `idle` display state is UNCHANGED — renaming a shipped display state to make room for a new word is a larger edit
  than choosing a different word.
- consequence: a reader who greps the tree for `idle` keeps finding display state, and that stays correct. The
  quiescence clock is greppable as `quiescence`, which returns the mechanism and nothing else.
- verify-machine: a grep guard asserting no quiescence topic or payload key contains the token `idle` other than
  `idle_seconds`, and that no quiescence plate string uses the word `idle`.
- verify-human: David reads both states on the glass in one session and never has to ask which sense is meant.
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

---

## 4. Boundaries and the acceptance bar

### [CXC-CQ-BOUNDARIES] Error and boundary conditions, stated so that none of them lands on the happy path

| # | Condition | Required behaviour |
|---|-----------|--------------------|
| 1 | no meta-object for the child's instance id | coded defaults apply, the attempted path is LOGGED, the readback `source` reads `coded-default` |
| 2 | a `HEARTBEAT_*_S` pin in the console host's env | the pin applies and the readback `source` reads `host-env` for every hosted child, so the sharing is visible |
| 3 | effective `base <= 0` | the heartbeat is DISABLED per the existing `heartbeat_interval` contract, and the readback says disabled rather than reading as a zero-second cadence |
| 4 | nothing bound behind the child's channel namespace | the heartbeat is NOT ARMED and the plate names the remedy ([DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD], ratified 2026-08-13); re-probed each poll, so a later bridge arms it with no relaunch |
| 5 | the publish raises outright | identical treatment to row 4 — a raising publish and a vanishing one are the same fact to the clock |
| 6 | the child's transcript cannot be resolved yet | quiescence still publishes; idle is measured from PTY output, not from the transcript, so it is available before context is |
| 7 | the echo returns later than `echo_grace_s` | the carried identifier still marks it as an echo and the clock still does not reset ([CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD]) |
| 8 | the widget unmounts while the child is quiet | the monitor thread stops with the session and the retained quiescence state is CLEARED, never left as a stale last reading |
| 9 | two children hosted in one console | two monitors, two topics, two clocks, and no shared cadence state beyond whatever [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS] rules |
| 10 | the human types at the glass and the child echoes | that echo counts as genuine activity exactly as it does for the launcher — unchanged, and deliberately so |

- constraint: rows 1, 2, 4 and 5 are the four that resolve SILENTLY today. That is what makes them the acceptance
  bar rather than documentation.
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-ACCEPTANCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [CXC-CQ-ACCEPTANCE] The two-tier bar — a machine signal per gap, and a glass gate for the three a human owns

| id | Criterion | Tier |
|----|-----------|------|
| CQ-A1 | the widget constructs the monitor with a non-None `publish_cadence`, and one readback carries the three values in MINUTES under the child's id | machine |
| CQ-A2 | a cadence edit reaches a RUNNING hosted child within one `poll_s` and the readback agrees, with no relaunch | machine and glass |
| CQ-A3 | a configured 10/60/60 and a defaulted 10/60/60 are distinguishable by the readback's `source` field | machine |
| CQ-A4 | a nudge that raises AND a nudge into an unbound namespace both leave the heartbeat clock UNADVANCED and both record a machine-readable condition | machine |
| CQ-A5 | crossing `backoff_after` emits exactly ONE quiescence publish, and no quiescence publish is emitted more often than `poll_s` | machine |
| CQ-A6 | continuous idle crosses `backoff_after` and the cadence escalates DESPITE an echo on every nudge, including an echo delayed past `echo_grace_s` | machine |
| CQ-A7 | zero PTY writes occur on any quiescence path, proven by a grep guard and by a fired-heartbeat test | machine |
| CQ-A8 | a human can read, from the glass alone, how long a hosted child has been quiet and whether the harness has backed off | glass |
| CQ-A9 | a nudge arrives in the child's transcript as an attributable message and does not disturb what the human is typing | glass |
| CQ-A10 | with no bridge bound the heartbeat is NOT armed and the plate names the remedy; a bridge appearing on a later poll arms it, and one disappearing disarms it, both with no relaunch | machine and glass |

- verify-machine: CQ-A1, CQ-A3, CQ-A4, CQ-A5, CQ-A6 and CQ-A7 are unit tests under an injected clock with a fake
  publish and no real bus; CQ-A2's machine half drives `cadence_provider` directly, and CQ-A10's drives a fake
  bridge probe that appears and disappears across polls.
- verify-human: CQ-A2's glass half, CQ-A8 and CQ-A9 are David at the console. CQ-A8 in particular cannot be closed
  by a test, because the failure it guards against is a readout that is present and unreadable.
- constraint: no criterion here is met by the code as measured on 2026-08-13. This spec's entire content is ten
  criteria that currently fail.
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-BOUNDARIES]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

---

## 5. The design forks — four OPEN, two settled

### [DD-CQ-MONITOR-LOCATION] WHERE the idle monitor lives — settled elsewhere, and WEAKENED by the split

- decision: SETTLED, and not re-opened by this spec. [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] was resolved by David on
  2026-08-11 in favour of an IN-PROCESS monitor thread, against its own weak recommendation for a standalone
  publisher, and it is BUILT — `TerminalWidget._start_monitor` starts `HarnessMonitor.start_thread()` and
  `stop_session` stops it.
- alternatives: "(a) IN-PROCESS, a monitor thread the widget owns, published through the console's client — lifetime is exactly the child's, no orphan to reap, no second flock, at the cost of per-child work inside the render process. (b) STANDALONE `self_publisher --namespace-by-id <child-id>` spawned beside the child — real isolation, nothing it does can cost a frame, and the child's telemetry survives a console bounce, at the cost of a second process whose lifetime must be tied to the child's or it orphans. (c) PUBLISH NOTHING and let the console read the child's transcript in its existing data layer — rejected in [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] because it puts transcript parsing for N children on the render process's critical path and makes the child's telemetry a console feature rather than the child's own signal." (a) was chosen; recorded here in full so this node is readable without opening the fork it cites.
- rationale: the deciding argument was lifetime. The thread's lifetime is exactly the child's, so there is no orphan
  to reap, no second flock and no daemon-launcher edit; an orphaned publisher republishing a dead child's last
  reading is a stale gauge that the in-process shape cannot produce.
- consequence: [CXC-CQ-SPLIT] WEAKENS the fork rather than reopening it. Once nudging is a channel message, the
  monitor needs only the READ side of the PTY, which the widget's reader thread already provides. The question
  shrinks from "which process owns the child's harness" to "which process observes the child's bytes", and only one
  process can answer that.
- consequence: every requirement in this spec therefore constrains code running INSIDE the render process, which is
  why [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE] carries a publish-on-edge rule and a `poll_s` floor rather than a publish-per-tick design.
  The cost that branch (b) was chosen against is the cost this spec must not incur.
- invalidating_assumption: "That the monitor's per-child work stays off the frame budget. If a measured frame cost
  is ever attributable to a hosted child's monitor thread — a wedged transcript parse, a blocking publish — then
  [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] branch (b) reopens on evidence and this spec's publish requirements move with it."
- status: decided
- see-also: [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-SPLIT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [DD-CQ-NUDGE-MESSAGE-SHAPE] What the nudge MESSAGE is — its namespace, its payload, and STATE versus EVENT

- decision: OPEN. [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT] settles that the nudge is a channel message rather than a keystroke.
  It does not settle the message's namespace, its payload, or its bus KIND, and the last of those is not cosmetic.
- constraint: (the live hazard) the bus dispatches STATE and EVENT to DIFFERENT subscriber sets —
  `bus.publish_state` and `bus.publish_event` are separate entry points with separate delivery semantics. Choosing
  the wrong one produces a message that publishes successfully and reaches nobody, which is the same silent shape
  as [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY]'s unbound namespace and is a live failure class in this codebase.
- alternatives: "(a) EVENT on the child's existing inbound channel — reuses `intelligence/<child-id>/in`, the exact
  address `channel.mjs` already subscribes, so the child cannot tell a widget nudge from a launcher nudge. Zero new
  namespace, and it inherits whatever that path's failure modes are. (b) EVENT on a dedicated quiescence namespace
  — `intelligence/<child-id>/quiescence/nudge`, separable, independently subscribable, greppable, and observable by
  a third party that is not the child. Requires the child's bridge to relay a second namespace. (c) STATE on a
  quiescence namespace — retained, so a child that reconnects sees the outstanding nudge. Wrong for a nudge that
  should expire, and a retained nudge that survives a reconnect is a message the child will answer twice."
- rationale: the argument for (a) is that it is the address that already works and already carries the launcher's
  nudges; the argument for (b) is that a nudge observable only by its recipient cannot be audited by anyone else,
  which forfeits reason (2) of [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT].
- recommendation: (weak) (a) for delivery plus (b) as an OBSERVATION copy — the child hears it on the address it
  already listens to, and the audit trail exists on a namespace anyone can subscribe. Two publishes, one act,
  clearly labelled which is which.
- consequence: whichever wins, the payload MUST carry sender, reason and the echo identifier
  [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD] requires. Those three fields are settled by other nodes and are not part of this
  fork.
- invalidating_assumption: "That a third party ever wants to observe a nudge. If nothing but the child and its own
  monitor ever reads it, the observation copy is unread traffic and (a) alone is correct — check whether any
  console surface or supervisor would bind a nudge namespace before building the second publish."
- status: proposed
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-READBACK]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [DD-CQ-BRIDGELESS-CHILD] What a hosted child does when nothing is bound behind its channel namespace — RATIFIED

- decision: RATIFIED (David, 2026-08-13) — **(a) REFUSE TO ARM.** No bridge, no idle monitor, and the glass says
  so. This is a DEVIATION from the architect's recommendation of (b) degrade-loudly, and David's reason is the
  better one, so it is recorded verbatim below rather than paraphrased into the argument it overturned.
- deviation: the architect recommended (b) ARM AND DEGRADE LOUDLY, on the grounds that measurement and nudging are
  separable ([CXC-CQ-SPLIT]), so losing the nudge should not cost the idle clock. That argument ASSUMED the child
  was otherwise a usable collaborator. It is not: a hosted child runs `--ax-screen-reader` ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]),
  a deliberately restricted surface, so the channel is not one of several ways to work with it — without the
  channel it is barely collaborable at all. The recommendation rested on a premise this deployment does not supply.
- claim: `refuse to arm` is not caution, it is REFUSING TO INSTRUMENT AN UNREACHABLE PEER. A child with no bridge
  is not a degraded child, it is a non-functional one, and arming an idle monitor on it is theatre — measuring the
  quiescence of something nobody can reach produces a number with no action attached to it, and the entire point of
  the two-tier heartbeat is to ACT on the idle rather than to record it.
- premise: (measured, 2026-08-13) the console runs ONE `channel_bridge`, bound to a single instance id. A peer
  whose id has no bridge behind it publishes into a namespace with nothing listening and the publish SUCCEEDS.
  There is no error, no drop counter and no signal of any kind.
- alternatives: "(a) REFUSE TO ARM — the widget checks for a bound bridge before starting the monitor's heartbeat
  half and, finding none, does not arm it and says so on the glass. Honest, and it means the absence of nudges is
  explained rather than mysterious; it also means a child whose bridge arrives later never gets nudged. (b) ARM AND
  DEGRADE LOUDLY — arm the heartbeat, attempt every nudge, and mark the undelivered condition on the glass and on
  the bus per [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY]. Self-healing when a bridge appears, at the cost of a widget that shows a fault
  condition for a configuration nobody intended to be an error. (c) FALL BACK — deliver the nudge by some other
  path when no bridge is bound. This is where a PTY keystroke would re-enter, and [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-TRANSPORT]'s
  four reasons argue against it as strongly here as anywhere."
- rationale: the choice turned on a question this spec could not answer from measurement — whether a bridgeless
  hosted child is a MISCONFIGURATION or a legitimate deployment. David answered it by naming a fact the spec had
  not weighed: the SURFACE. `--ax-screen-reader` makes the channel the only practical way to work with the child,
  so a bridgeless child is neither a misconfiguration to tolerate nor a deployment to support — it is unreachable,
  and (a) is right and loud. The lesson worth keeping is that the deciding evidence was a property of the terminal
  mode, not of the heartbeat, and no amount of reasoning about the monitor would have surfaced it.
- recommendation: (weak, NOT TAKEN — recorded because a superseded recommendation is evidence about the reasoning,
  not noise) (b), because it degrades rather than refuses and because [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY] already requires the
  machine-readable condition it depends on.
- requirement: the widget MUST probe for a bound bridge on the child's namespace BEFORE arming the monitor's
  heartbeat half, and MUST NOT arm it when none is bound. With the heartbeat unarmed there is no quiescence tier
  and no cadence, so [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE] publishes the REFUSAL in place of a reading rather than a tier nobody is
  maintaining. `_on_child_bytes` already no-ops its `note_activity()` call when `self._monitor is None`
  (terminal.py lines 484-489), so the unarmed shape needs no new branch on the byte path.
- requirement: (the refusal names the REMEDY, not the condition) the glass MUST carry what to DO, not only what is
  wrong. "no bridge for `<child-id>`" is a symptom; "start a `channel_bridge` for `<child-id>`" is actionable. The
  fix is a known one-line change rather than a mystery, so reporting only the symptom is a choice, not a limit —
  and a refusal that does not say what to do is one people learn to ignore, which turns a loud signal into a silent
  one by habit instead of by code.
- requirement: (RE-CHECKABLE, never one-shot) the probe MUST be re-evaluated on the monitor's existing poll, so a
  bridge that appears later ARMS the heartbeat and clears the plate with no relaunch, and a bridge that disappears
  DISARMS it rather than emitting nudges nobody receives. A sticky refusal makes someone restart a whole console to
  clear a condition that has already resolved itself.
- consequence: [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY]'s clock rule survives and NARROWS. With the heartbeat unarmed there is no clock to
  advance, so the undelivered-nudge case shrinks to a bridge that was bound at arm time and vanished before the
  next poll — which the re-check converts into a disarm instead of a stream of undelivered nudges.
- consequence: this STRENGTHENS [CXC-CQ-SPLIT] rather than weakening it. Measurement and nudging remain separable
  IN THE ARCHITECTURE; the ruling is that in THIS deployment, on THIS surface, measurement without nudging has no
  consumer. The split keeps its value for the next host — one not screen-reader-restricted — and this node records
  why its independent halves are not independently USEFUL here.
- scenario:
  - when: a hosted child is spawned and no bridge is bound behind its namespace
  - then: the heartbeat is NOT armed, and the plate names both the condition and the remedy
  - when: a bridge is started for that child while the widget is still mounted
  - then: the next poll arms the heartbeat and the plate clears, with no relaunch
  - when: the bridge disappears while the heartbeat is armed
  - then: the next poll DISARMS it and the plate returns, rather than firing nudges nobody receives
- verify-machine: a test asserts no heartbeat is armed when the bridge probe reports none bound; a test asserts the
  plate string carries the remedy and not only the condition; a test appears-then-disappears the bridge across
  polls and asserts arm and disarm both happen with no relaunch.
- verify-human: David spawns a hosted child with no bridge, reads the plate, does what it names, and watches the
  monitor arm itself without a relaunch.
- invalidating_assumption: "That a hosted child is only collaborable through the channel. This ruling rests on
  `--ax-screen-reader` being V1's target mode ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]); if the target mode changes to one a human
  can work with directly at the glass, a bridgeless child stops being non-functional and (b) degrade-loudly becomes
  correct again. Anyone changing the terminal mode MUST reopen this node."
- invalidating_assumption: "That one bridge per console is the durable topology. If the console gains a bridge per
  hosted child, or the bridge learns to bind ANNOUNCED namespaces rather than a configured id, the bridgeless case
  stops existing and this ruling closes as not-applicable. That second option is [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] applied
  one level up, which is worth checking before building the probe."
- status: decided
- dsd-source: docs/intelligence-console/DECISIONS-2026-08-13-crossing-primer-and-publication.md
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-DELIVERY]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-SPLIT]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE]
- see-also: [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS] Which of the three identity axes owns a hosted child's quiescence config and namespace

- decision: OPEN. A hosted instance has three axes — ROLE selects the artifacts it orients from, INSTANCE ID is what
  it is called on topics and telemetry, HOST is where it renders. Telemetry is already ruled to the INSTANCE axis by
  [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID], which is why [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE] keys the quiescence topic on the child's id and treats that
  as settled. The CONFIG axis is not ruled, and today it resolves from a mixture of all three by accident.
- alternatives: "(a) INSTANCE — each hosted child carries its own `lifecycle.heartbeat`, edited per child. Most
  precise, and it creates one meta-object per spawn, most of which will never be edited. (b) ROLE — the cadence
  lives on the role manifest the child orients from, so every `composeux-architect` child shares one cadence. This
  matches how the role already selects artifacts, and it makes a per-child override impossible without a fourth
  mechanism. (c) HOST — the console's own config sets the cadence for everything it hosts, which is what the env pin
  does today by accident. Simplest and least defensible, because it makes a host-axis value beat an instance-axis
  one."
- rationale: the `source` field required by [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-IDENTITY] is what makes any of these three honest, and
  it is required regardless of which wins — which is why it is a requirement and this is a fork.
- recommendation: (weak) (b) with (a) as an override. The role is the axis that already carries every other
  orientation artifact, and a per-instance override costs one lookup rather than one file per spawn.
- consequence: whichever wins, the HOST env pin must stop being an unannounced fourth axis. Under (a) or (b) it
  becomes an explicit operator override that the readback names as `host-env`.
- invalidating_assumption: "That a hosted child's cadence is ever edited at all. If in practice every hosted child
  runs at the coded default forever, the axis question is academic and the fork collapses into 'the default is the
  config' — check whether any cadence value has ever been persisted for a non-launcher instance before building
  either branch."
- status: proposed
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-CADENCE-IDENTITY]
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [DD-CQ-CONSTANTS] Whether 10 / 60 / 60 minutes transfers to a hosted child, and on what measurement

- decision: OPEN, and explicitly not to be settled by taste. The current effective hosted default is
  `600 s / 3600 s / 3600 s`, inherited wholesale from a launcher serving a session David talks to directly. A
  widget-hosted child may be a bounded worker nobody is waiting on, or a long-lived peer, and those want different
  numbers. `echo_grace_s = 90` rides the same question and is now stretched across a transport boundary
  ([CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD]), so it is in scope here rather than in a fork of its own.
- alternatives: "(a) INHERIT — one cadence for every host, which is what ships today and is defensible precisely
  because it is one number to reason about. (b) MEASURE THEN SET — instrument the hosted children that already run,
  collect the distribution of genuine-quiet intervals over a week, and set `base` below the interval at which a
  nudge would have helped and `backoff_after` above the interval at which nudging is noise. (c) PER-ROLE —
  different cadences for different roles, which presupposes [DD-CQ-CADENCE-AXIS] resolving to (b) or to (a) with an
  override."
- rationale: the predecessor's own numbers were not chosen from a measurement either. 10/60/60 arrived as David's
  Config-tab values after a single-tier 600 failed. Repeating that derivation for a different host would be
  repeating a guess and calling it inheritance.
- recommendation: (weak) (a) NOW and (b) LATER — ship the inherited cadence so nothing is blocked, and make
  [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE]'s publication the instrument that (b) needs. The quiescence topic this spec requires IS the
  measurement apparatus for this fork, which is an argument for building it before deciding.
- consequence: choosing (b) makes this spec's observability work a PREREQUISITE rather than an improvement, and the
  decision cannot be taken until the data exists.
- invalidating_assumption: "That a hosted child benefits from being nudged at all. If a quiescent hosted child is
  simply finished, the right cadence is 'never' and the correct behaviour is to report it quiet and let a human or
  a supervisor reap it — at which point this is a lifecycle question, not a cadence question."
- status: proposed
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-OBSERVABLE]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD]
- see-also: [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

### [DD-CQ-PEER-INJECT-IS-ACTIVITY] Whether a PEER's bus inject counts as genuine activity or as an echo

- decision: OPEN, and currently answered by accident. `HarnessMonitor._emit` stamps `_last_self_emit`, so the
  child's reply to a HARNESS message is discounted as echo. `PtyChild.inject()` — the path a bus peer's keystroke
  takes — does not stamp it. A peer injecting into a hosted child therefore produces child output that resets the
  idle clock to the fast tier, exactly as a human keystroke would.
- alternatives: "(a) GENUINE — a peer nudged it and it responded, which is real work, so the clock should reset.
  (b) ECHO — a peer's inject is machine-originated the same way a harness nudge is, and discounting it keeps idle
  meaning 'nothing external caused this'. (c) DEPENDS ON THE ORIGIN — a human-originated inject relayed by a peer
  is genuine and a machine-originated one is echo, which requires the envelope to carry an origin field it does not
  carry today."
- rationale: the launcher never faced this because its injects were its own. The convergence [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]
  built — a bus keystroke and a keyboard keystroke are indistinguishable to the child BY CONSTRUCTION — is exactly
  what makes the question unanswerable at the fd and forces it up to the envelope.
- recommendation: (weak) (a), because it is what ships, because it is the conservative direction (over-counting
  activity produces fewer nudges, not more), and because (c) needs an origin field nothing currently carries.
- consequence: if (b) or (c) wins, `_emit` stops being the single stamping chokepoint and the echo window becomes a
  property of the write path rather than of the harness — a change to `ptycore`, not to the widget.
- invalidating_assumption: "That peers inject into hosted children at all. If the only writers are the human at the
  glass and the child's own harness, this fork has no subject and should be closed as not-applicable rather than
  decided."
- status: proposed
- see-also: [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]
- see-also: [CXC-CQ-NUDGE-ECHO-GUARD]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CHILD-QUIESCENCE]

